Forth dictionaries (*.dic) -- Sun's BSD+ grant, by our reading
--------------------------------------------------------------
The seven prebuilt Forth dictionaries under src/obp:

    obp/os/sun/sparc/{forth,forth32-xref,f32t32}.dic
    obp/fm/kernel/sparc/{kernel-xref,k32t32-xref,k64t32}.dic
    obp/pkg/tokenizr/sparc/tokenize.dic

kernel-xref.dic is also what the bootstrap that avoids forth.dic starts from
(forth_bootstrap/README). `make bootstrap-check` derives it from the sources in
src/obp by Sun's metacompiler on cforth and compares it with this file; no
copy of it is kept outside src/obp.

All seven are binary and carry no header (LICENSE_SRC.MAP: none stated). We
redistribute them under Sun's BSD+ grant, which says "source and binary
forms": the OpenSPARC T2 release carries one licence text for it
(BSD+_License.txt), and its BINARY_SLA.txt names other binaries (the SAM
binaries in the directory legion, Solaris 10), not the obp directory. That is
our reading, not a statement in the files. No file from the directory legion
and no Solaris binary is in this tree. forth.dic is the root and has no
source of its own; the other six are rebuilt from Forth source in src/obp on
every build, starting from it, and the shipped copies are not read
(build/obp/README).
The chain is drawn in docs/obp-build-flow.png.


CDDL in src/hypervisor/src/support/stabs/
------------------------------------------
Seven files there (asmcheck.c, forth.c, genassym.c, main.c, squander.c, stabs.c,
stabs.h) carry Sun's BSD+ header and, after it, a CDDL 1.0 header
(LICENSE_SRC.MAP: class BC). They build the `stabs` tool, which only the sun
path runs (it makes offsets.h). The gnu path neither builds nor uses them, and
no firmware file contains their code. Both headers are kept as they are; which
of the two governs matters only to someone who changes these files, and does not
touch the firmware. No other CDDL material is in the current tree.


md/ machine descriptions -- GPL-2.0-only
-----------------------------------------
Everything under md/ that is a source of the MDs is GPL-2.0-only. The full
text, copied verbatim from the release archive, is
md/GPLv2_License_OpenSPARCT1.txt. 1up-md.bin and 1up-hv.bin are compiled from
these files, so they are GPL-2 object code; their source is md/ at the commit
that built them (the banner and the build stamp name it).

Where the files come from:

1. md/common.hdesc, md/common.pdesc, md/{legion,pci}/1up.pdesc
   Sun's OpenSPARC T1 MD configurations (T1-archive: legion/src/config/niagara/
   and hypervisor/src/greatlakes/ontario/t1_fpga/configs/). Unlike the BSD+
   hypervisor and OpenBoot code they are GPL-2, and each file keeps Sun's
   header saying so. Our changes are under the same terms: above Sun's header
   each file has an SPDX line and a "Modified 2026" line with our copyright
   (the AUTHORS file).

2. Written here, following T2-archive files that name no licence
   (header: "Use is subject to license terms"):
     - md/{legion,pci}/1up.hdesc: written to what the hypervisor
       reads, in the layout of
       T2-archive:t1_fpga/src/xilinx/configs/*_hv.desc; six structural lines
       are shared with Sun's T1 1up.hdesc. They are compiled together with the
       GPL-2 md/common.hdesc into 1up-hv.bin;
     - the iodevice and interrupt-map-entry nodes of md/pci: most property
       lines of the iodevice node are those of
       T2-archive:t1_fpga/src/xilinx/configs/1c1t_guest.desc.
   The values are set by code: the hypervisor (BSD+), FWARC 2007/386, the
   OpenBoot code and the OS px (PCIe) driver code, or the address map of QEMU
   (md/README and md/REFERENCE give them value by value). We do not claim that
   no value was taken from a Sun file.
   These T2-archive files (t1_fpga/src/xilinx/configs/*_hv.desc and
   1c1t_guest.desc) name no licence. Their header says "Use is subject to
   license terms" and that they are derived from machine description binaries
   in the OpenSPARC T2 legion simulation directory. What our files share with
   them is what the hypervisor, the OS px (PCIe) driver, OpenBoot and the
   public specifications (FWARC 2007/386, Hypervisor API) require: the names
   of the nodes and properties and their values, written as "name = value;".
   The comments, the macros and the structure of our files are ours.
   So our files are GPL-2.0-only, each with an SPDX line and our copyright line,
   and we hold that this is enough; these files give us no other terms to
   follow.


Everything else in this repository -- BSD-2-Clause, with one exception
------------------------------------------------------------------------
build/, patches/ (the patch text itself, as distinct from the BSD+ code it
patches), scripts/, container/, tests/, forth_bootstrap/,
build/hv_reset/gnu/abi/ (sys/stack.h, written here from the SPARC Compliance
Definition 2.4.1), md/README, and the prose files (README,
REQUIREMENTS.*, this file) are this project's own work, under the BSD
2-Clause licence (LICENSE.BSD-2-Clause). Their holder is
named in the AUTHORS file; scripts and makefiles carry a two-line header
saying so (`make headers-check`). A patch merged in place into a BSD+ file
takes the terms of that file.

Exception: material from Sun inside that layer keeps Sun's BSD+ terms
(BSD+_License_OpenSPARCT2.txt, copied verbatim from the OpenSPARC T2 release,
OpenSPARCT2_Arch.1.2.tar.bz2); what this project added to it is BSD-2-Clause:

    build/obp/gnu/obp-gnu-inner.mk         one rule copied from Sun's makefile
    build/obp/gnu/openboot.ld              translation of Sun's linker mapfile
    build/hv_reset/gnu/reference/          offsets generated from Sun's build

The last two carry Sun's notice to be safe: openboot.ld in a header of its own, reference/ in reference/NOTICE (its two files
are compared byte for byte with generated output, so they cannot hold one). obp-gnu-inner.mk names the rule it took from Sun in its header.
